FOUND: What is stored: the address, which emails and topics you chose, the time it was added, and when it was confirmed or unsubscribed, in the ERW's Supabase database; an address that unsubscribes is also kept on a suppression list, so that it is never emailed again.
FOUND: The site's server writes the address with the warehouse's service key, which can read the table and which no browser is given; no page of the site reads an address back.
FOUND: What the email is Weekdays (no weekend issue): the day's Energy Digest in short: its opening number; its numbers (a market price, a physical figure such as demand, a project, and a policy, capital or reliability figure); the first three stories of what moved, each with why it matters and a link to its source; the chart of the day and a fun fact.
FOUND: Sundays, 23:00 UTC (4 PM Pacific in summer time, 3 PM in winter): the ERW's Roundup : its opening number, the week in five numbers, the first three stories of what mattered, the chart of the week, the week ahead and a fun fact; the weekend's stories and the rest of the week are one link away on the site.
FOUND: Each figure says where it came from: a warehouse table, as on this site, or, for a figure a news story reported, its outlet.
FOUND: Your topics: with every topic chosen, the email's stories are the first three of the issue's own; with fewer, up to five of the day's (or week's) highest-ranked stories in the topics you choose.
FOUND: A model (Claude Haiku 4.5, the one model fixed for scoring since 12 October 2026; the digest's other steps use the newest Sonnet-class model the API lists) scores every story against a fixed rubric ( warehouse/news/rubric.md ): significance from 0 to 10 across the whole energy industry, a sector, a region, and a one-line reason.
FOUND: Sundays, 23:00 UTC (4 PM Pacific in summer time, 3 PM in winter): the ERW's Roundup : its opening number, the week in five numbers, the first three stories of what mattered, the chart of the week, the week ahead and a fun fact; the weekend's stories and the rest of the week are one link away on the site.
FOUND: ERW's Roundup covers an ISO week (Monday to Sunday, UTC), opens with its opening number and the week in five numbers (the weekend's stories are under More), and is written and sent on Sundays at 23:00 UTC (4 PM Pacific in summer time; 3 PM from early November to mid-March, when the Pacific coast keeps standard time).
FOUND: Every email then carries a signed unsubscribe link ( warehouse/news/email_digest.py ): it opens this page, and one press of its button stops every email (the link alone changes nothing, so a mail scanner that follows it cannot unsubscribe you); a mail program that shows its own unsubscribe button stops them in one click.
FOUND: Sign up Email address Send me the daily Energy Digest ERW's Roundup, on Sundays Topics for the top stories (all are checked; uncheck what you do not want) Power prices Gas and LNG Oil Nuclear Renewables and storage Transmission and grid Datacenters and AI power Deals and capital Policy Geopolitics Leave empty Sign up Privacy: your address is used only to send the emails you chose and is never sold; it is shared with no one but Resend, the service that sends the emails (the confirmation and every issue), and you can ask for it to be removed at any time.
FOUND: The digest covers the news since the previous issue was written, at most 96 hours back (about 24 hours on a weekday after a weekday, the weekend in Monday's issue), Monday to Friday, by the daily run that starts at 14:00 UTC; there is no weekend issue.
FOUND: ERW's Roundup covers an ISO week (Monday to Sunday, UTC), opens with its opening number and the week in five numbers (the weekend's stories are under More), and is written and sent on Sundays at 23:00 UTC (4 PM Pacific in summer time; 3 PM from early November to mid-March, when the Pacific coast keeps standard time).
FOUND: Coal has a layer since 19 November 2026, the coal fields.
MISSING: since then coal is a layer, and only uranium and critical minerals are greyed
FOUND: The queue rows of the table energy_projects in the live set, which holds requests that are not withdrawn, from the queue reports of these grids: ERCOT, SPP, CAISO, ISO-NE.
FOUND: EIA-930 hourly demand, solar and wind from January 2019, and battery output from November 2024 (EIA's series of it begins on 23 October 2024).
FOUND: Demand: an hour of demand that could not have happened (zero, more than a quarter away from the four hours around it, or outside one third to three times the grid's median hour) is a blank, and its day is not a complete day.
